id: records/2026-09-05-selectable-aperture-and-provisioning-clarification
title: "One Yawn, many selectable apertures, explicit provisioning"
kind: clarification-record
status: proposed-canonical-backfill
recorded: 2026-09-05
supersedes_history: false
mutationAuthorized: false
externalEffectsAuthorized: false

source_observation: >
  Dave clarified that the system should not always compress a Yawn to the same
  small surface. The user should control the aperture through a visible dropdown,
  have sensible private and public defaults, and be able to add other Views over
  the same .yawn model. YAWN may detect that another representation could fit the
  present task or accepted preference better, but should propose that change
  rather than hard-code a fixed learning type. Nestheads may be one open-world
  game aperture over the same underlying model.

preserve:
  - >
    The underlying Yawn should contain or reference enough attributed semantic
    state, relationships, events, provenance, preferences, grants, and source
    coordinates to reconstruct permitted cognitive projections.
  - >
    Different people may need different apertures over the same model, and the
    same person may need different apertures for different questions, devices,
    moments, or purposes.
  - >
    Questions First is a strong default for sparse orientation, but the user must
    be able to choose another compatible aperture or use Auto.
  - >
    A private owner View and a public published View may coexist without
    duplicating the canonical Yawn.
  - >
    Provisioning should support fast invitation by username or email while
    retaining explicit access levels, expiration, revocation, and auditability.

correct:
  - >
    Aperture and View are related but not identical. The existing ontology uses
    aperture for scope, depth, time window, and salience inside a View. The UI
    may call a saved View-plus-aperture-plus-presentation binding an Aperture,
    while machine records remain explicit.
  - >
    The .yawn package need not inline every raw source body. Sensitive or large
    sources may remain local, encrypted, external, expired, or deleted; the
    canonical package preserves the permitted state, coordinates, hashes,
    status, and access policy needed for honest reconstruction.
  - >
    Provisioning cannot belong only to the aperture. Rights must protect the
    canonical resources and source fields first. A published aperture is an
    additional authorized disclosure boundary and can only narrow what those
    grants allow.
  - >
    Public and private should not be separate truth stores. They are separately
    configured projections of one canonical Yawn, potentially pinned to
    different revisions.
  - >
    User preferences are real and useful, but a persistent "visual learner" or
    similar diagnosis should not be inferred. Store scoped accepted preferences
    and measured local utility instead.

interface_target:
  header: >
    The Yawnbot title surface should place time/replay, Aperture, and
    provisioning controls next to the address so the user can immediately see
    when, how, and for whom the Yawn is being rendered.
  example: >
    [Health / dave/health] [Now v] [Aperture: Questions First v]
    [Private: only Dave v]
  aperture_menu:
    - Auto
    - Questions First
    - Attention Frontier
    - Relationship Inspector
    - Concept Map
    - Timeline / Replay
    - Proof / Evidence
    - Decisions / Moves
    - Raw Model
    - Agentic Yawnbot
    - Public Story
    - Nestheads Game World
    - Custom or imported apertures
  note: >
    The menu is registry-driven. The list above is an initial candidate set, not
    a closed hard-coded enum.

preference_scopes:
  - this_turn_only
  - this_view_or_shared_link
  - this_yawn
  - this_arena
  - this_agent_space
  - this_principal_private_default
  - this_principal_public_authoring_default
  - system_fallback

preference_learning:
  explicit_signals:
    - selected_aperture
    - saved_scope
    - direct_feedback
    - requested_density_or_medium
    - accessibility_requirement
  supporting_signals:
    - correction_cost
    - task_success
    - navigation_success
    - context_restoration
    - repeated_switches
  insufficient_alone:
    - clicks
    - dwell_time
    - session_length
    - emotional_engagement
  behavior: >
    YAWN may say, "This appears easier to inspect as a relationship map because
    three open questions depend on the same relationship. Switch for now?" It
    must show the reason and preserve immediate override.

provisioning_layers:
  canonical_resource_access: >
    Who may discover, read, comment, propose, edit, share, export, connect
    intelligence, equip capabilities, authorize effects, or administer each
    Yawn, field, relationship, source, evidence item, and descendant scope.
  aperture_publication: >
    Which named View/aperture and revision may be rendered to a given audience,
    with which fields, sanitization, link rules, expiration, and export rights.
  provider_connection: >
    Which data an intelligence provider may receive, retain, process, export, or
    use, independently of the human collaborator's access level.
  effect_authority: >
    Which operations an Agent may perform. Read or edit access does not
    automatically grant real-world effect authority.

safe_defaults:
  owner: full_private_access
  new_yawn_visibility: private
  public_template: may_exist_but_unpublished
  public_discoverability: false_until_explicit_publication
  external_agent_access: deny_until_explicit_grant
  inherited_rights: may_narrow_never_widen

public_private_model:
  owner_private_default: >
    The owner chooses or accepts a private default aperture. The system may use
    Questions First for sparse Yawns and Attention Frontier for a root Agent
    Space, subject to context and user override.
  public_default: >
    A Yawn may have a named Public Story or other public aperture, but it remains
    disabled until explicitly provisioned. Public viewers see the published
    projection, not the owner's private default.
  shared_viewer: >
    Invited users may choose their own permitted presentation aperture while
    contributions and annotations remain separately attributed and provisioned.

nestheads_relationship:
  statement: >
    Nestheads can be treated as an embodied open-world aperture over .yawn rather
    than another competing ontology. The Phaser world renders canonical symbols,
    relationships, Yawnbot bodies, state, and movement through game metaphors.
  invariants:
    - same_canonical_ids
    - same_event_cursor
    - same_authority_and_provisioning
    - same_proof_boundary
    - accessible_non_game_equivalent
    - game_action_requires_explicit_semantic_operator_before_state_mutation

codebase_findings:
  view_switcher: >
    The current product hard-codes public, dashboard, model, mapping,
    experiments, and history in a ViewMode union and fixed option array. That is
    a useful prototype but not the desired extensible meta-architecture.
  visibility_dropdown: >
    The current component presents public, participants, and private and has a
    public component default. New Yawns and unresolved effective rights should
    default to private/deny and derive access from grants.
  participants_dropdown: >
    The UI already points toward email/@username invitations, but non-AI user
    invitation is incomplete and displayed access is largely static. It must be
    backed by real invitations, roles, expiration, revocation, and audit events.
  visibility_resolver: >
    The current owner/participant/anonymous plus full/summary/hidden resolver is
    too coarse for the proposed field-, source-, View-, Agent-, and action-level
    model, but can remain as a compatibility adapter during migration.
  existing_strength: >
    projection-preference.v1 already models principal, scope, view kind, field
    path, set/reset, accepted status, revision, source event, and timestamps.
    Extend that substrate rather than introducing another preference store.

research_boundary: >
  Treat adaptive aperture selection as a testable interface policy. Respect
  user preferences and accessibility while measuring task-context usefulness.
  Do not claim a fixed learning-style classification or assume that matching a
  preferred medium necessarily improves learning.

first_test:
  source_yawn: one_real_private_Dave_Yawn
  same_underlying_state: true
  compare:
    - questions_first
    - attention_frontier
    - relationship_or_map_view
    - owner_selected_view
    - auto_suggested_view
    - nestheads_or_agentic_embodiment_when_available
  require:
    - stable_ids_and_meaning
    - no_semantic_mutation_on_switch
    - no_access_change_on_switch
    - visible_reason_for_auto_selection
    - cheap_user_override
    - distinct_private_and_public_projection
    - zero_private_field_leakage
    - measured_orientation_and_correction_cost

